 meaningfully behind after the first round of voting earlier this month.</s=
pan></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0=
 0 15px"><span>Some 10,000 kilometers northeast, voters in Serbia were also=
 taking part in an election that to many seemed, if not a foregone conclusi=
on, a pretty safe bet.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,=
serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>On the one hand, it was Serbia=E2=
=80=99s most competitive vote in years, thanks to the student protests over=
 corruption that had been ongoing since 2024. On the other, Serbia=E2=80=99=
s longtime nationalist strongman, Aleksandar Vucic, had dominated Serbian p=
olitics since 2012. He also had a firm grip on the police, nearly all major=
 media outlets and the electoral commission. Surely he would win again.</sp=
an></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 =
0 15px"><span>As it happens, these predictions were most likely also the pr=
eferred outcomes of Donald Trump, a Bolsonaro ally, and Vladimir Putin, a V=
ucic ally.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:=
